::PC_U01_L03_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
What is the domain of f(x) = sqrt(x + 3)
{
~A. x <= -3#This reverses the inequality. The radicand must be at least 0.
~B. all real numbers#The square root creates a restriction on the radicand.
=C. x >= -3#Correct. The radicand x + 3 must be nonnegative, so x >= -3.
~D. x >= 3#This uses the wrong sign when solving x + 3 >= 0.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
Which value is excluded from the domain of r(x) = 2 / (x - 9)
{
~A. 2#The numerator does not create the excluded input.
~B. 0#At x = 0, the denominator is -9, not 0.
=C. 9#Correct. x - 9 cannot equal 0.
~D. -9#This is the wrong sign. The denominator is zero when x = 9.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
For f(x) = sqrt(x - 1) + 4, what is the least possible output value
{
=A. 4#Correct. The square root part is at least 0, so the least output is 4.
~B. all real numbers#The range is restricted because square root outputs begin at a minimum value.
~C. -1#This confuses the input boundary with the output value.
~D. 1#This is the starting input, not the starting output.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
What vertical asymptote is indicated by g(x) = 3 / (x + 7)
{
=C. x = -7#Correct. x + 7 = 0 at x = -7.
~A. x = 7#This uses the wrong sign when setting the denominator equal to 0.
~B. y = -7#A vertical asymptote is written using x, not y.
~D. y = 7#This gives the wrong variable and sign.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
Which expression must be nonnegative in f(x) = sqrt(2x - 10)
{
~A. 2x#This is only part of the radicand and does not include the full expression.
=D. 2x - 10#Correct. The expression under the radical must be nonnegative.
~B. -10#The constant alone does not determine the domain.
~C. sqrt(x)#This is not the expression inside the given radical.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q06::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
What is the domain of h(x) = (x - 2) / (x + 5)
{
~A. all real numbers except x = 2#The numerator being zero does not restrict the domain.
~B. x >= -5#This is a radical-style restriction, not a rational denominator restriction.
~D. all real numbers#The denominator cannot equal zero, so one value is excluded.
=C. all real numbers except x = -5#Correct. x + 5 cannot equal 0.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q07::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
What is the starting point of f(x) = sqrt(x - 6)
{
~A. (0, -6)#This does not make the radicand zero.
~B. (-6, 0)#This uses the wrong sign for the horizontal shift.
~C. (0, 6)#This switches input and output information.
=D. (6, 0)#Correct. The radicand is zero when x = 6, and the output is 0.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q08::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
For r(x) = 1 / x, why is x = 0 not allowed
{
~B. The numerator is zero.#The numerator is 1, so it is not zero.
~C. The graph has no outputs at all.#The function has outputs for many inputs, but not for x = 0.
=A. Division by zero is undefined.#Correct. The denominator cannot equal zero.
~D. Square roots cannot be negative.#This is a rational function issue, not a radical restriction.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q09::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
Which function has domain x >= 2
{
=A. f(x) = sqrt(x - 2)#Correct. x - 2 >= 0 gives x >= 2.
~B. f(x) = sqrt(x + 2)#This has domain x >= -2.
~C. f(x) = sqrt(2 - x)#This has domain x <= 2.
~D. f(x) = 1 / (x - 2)#This excludes x = 2 but allows values on both sides.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q10::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
Which denominator gives a vertical asymptote at x = 4
{
~A. 4x#This denominator is zero at x = 0.
~C. x + 1#This denominator is zero at x = -1.
=B. x - 4#Correct. x - 4 = 0 when x = 4.
~D. x + 4#This denominator is zero at x = -4.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q11::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
If f(x) = sqrt(x) models distance from time, which input is not meaningful
{
~A. 12#Positive time values are allowed.
~B. 0#Time 0 is meaningful and gives distance 0 in this model.
=D. -2#Correct. Negative time is not meaningful in this context and is outside the radical domain.
~C. 5#Positive time values are meaningful.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q12::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
Which feature is usually associated with a denominator becoming zero in a rational function
{
~B. Minimum value#A zero denominator does not identify a minimum.
~C. y-intercept only#A y-intercept is found at x = 0 when defined.
~D. Square root endpoint#That is associated with radical functions.
=A. Vertical asymptote or removable hole#Correct. Denominator zeros can create vertical asymptotes or holes depending on the structure.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q13::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
Solve the domain inequality for f(x) = sqrt(3x + 12).
{
~A. x <= -4#This reverses the inequality after solving.
=C. x >= -4#Correct. 3x + 12 >= 0 gives x >= -4.
~B. x >= 4#This uses the wrong sign.
~D. all real numbers#The radicand must be nonnegative, so the domain is restricted.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q14::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
Which value is excluded from r(x) = (x + 3) / (x² - 16)
{
~A. 3#The numerator being zero does not restrict the domain.
=B. 4#Correct. x = 4 makes x² - 16 equal 0.
~C. 0#At x = 0, the denominator is -16, not 0.
~D. 16#The denominator is zero when x is 4 or -4, not 16.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q15::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
What is the range of f(x) = sqrt(x + 1) - 3
{
~A. all real numbers#The square root function has a lower bound after the shift.
~C. y >= 1#This confuses the input shift with the output shift.
=B. y >= -3#Correct. The square root part is at least 0, then the function shifts down 3.
~D. y <= -3#The square root graph opens upward from its starting output.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q16::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
For r(x) = 6 / (x - 1), what happens as x gets very close to 1
{
~A. The function becomes exactly 0.#The numerator stays 6, and the denominator approaches 0.
~B. The function is defined at x = 1.#The denominator is zero at x = 1, so the function is not defined there.
~C. The domain becomes all real numbers.#The excluded value remains part of the domain restriction.
=D. The output changes without bound near the vertical asymptote.#Correct. Values grow very large in magnitude near the zero denominator.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q17::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
Which graph feature belongs to f(x) = sqrt(x - 9)
{
=D. Endpoint at (9, 0)#Correct. The radical begins where x - 9 = 0.
~A. Vertical asymptote at x = 9#Radical endpoints are not vertical asymptotes.
~B. Endpoint at (-9, 0)#This uses the wrong sign.
~C. Hole at x = 9#A hole is associated with some rational functions, not this radical endpoint.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q18::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
What is the first step to find excluded values for r(x) = (x + 2) / (x² - 9)
{
~A. Set the whole function equal to one.#That solves a different equation.
=D. Set the denominator equal to zero.#Correct. Excluded values occur where the denominator is zero.
~B. Square both sides.#There is no radical equation requiring squaring.
~C. Set the numerator equal to zero.#That finds x-intercepts, not excluded domain values.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q19::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
What is the domain of f(x) = sqrt(10 - x)
{
~A. all real numbers#The even-index radical restricts the domain.
~B. x >= 10#This reverses the inequality. The radicand 10 - x must be nonnegative.
~D. x > 10#Inputs greater than 10 make the radicand negative.
=C. x <= 10#Correct. 10 - x >= 0 gives x <= 10.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q20::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
Which statement is true about r(x) = (x - 2) / (x - 2)
{
~A. x = 2 is allowed because the factors match.#The original denominator is zero at x = 2, so the input is excluded.
~B. The function has no restriction.#The denominator still creates an excluded value in the original function.
~C. The y-intercept is excluded because y = 2.#Domain restrictions concern x-values, not y-values.
=D. x = 2 is excluded even though the expression simplifies elsewhere.#Correct. The original denominator cannot be zero, so x = 2 is not in the domain.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q21::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
Which first step best supports a solution for Radical and Rational Function Models
{
~B. Skip directly to an answer without naming the given information.#Skipping the setup often leads to using the wrong method.
=A. Identify the given representation, quantities, and restrictions before calculating.#Correct. A strong Precalculus solution begins by identifying the representation and restrictions so the method matches the task.
~C. Use any formula from a different unit.#A different unit may use different assumptions or tools.
~D. Base the answer only on how a graph or expression appears.#Appearance can be misleading unless it is supported by values or relationships.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q22::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
What evidence should a student use when explaining Radical and Rational Function Models
{
=A. Use labeled values, equations, graphs, or tables from the problem as evidence.#Correct. Evidence must come from the given mathematical representation, not from a guess or unrelated rule.
~B. Ignore labels and units because they are not part of the calculation.#Labels and units help determine what the quantities mean.
~C. Use a future topic even when the current lesson gives enough information.#The current lesson evidence should be used before reaching for later topics.
~D. Use the longest answer choice as evidence.#Answer length is not mathematical evidence.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q23::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
Which response best shows mastery of Radical and Rational Function Models
{
=A. Show the steps, connect them to the lesson relationship, and interpret the result.#Correct. Mastery requires both accurate work and an interpretation connected to the lesson relationship.
~B. Change the given values to make the computation easier.#Changing given values changes the problem.
~C. Give only a final number with no reasoning.#A final number alone does not show how the relationship was used.
~D. Copy vocabulary without applying it to the problem.#Vocabulary must be connected to the actual representation or calculation.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q24::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
What should be checked before finalizing work involving Radical and Rational Function Models
{
~A. Check only that the answer choice is short.#Length does not show correctness.
~C. Remove restrictions because they make the problem harder.#Restrictions are part of the mathematical meaning.
~D. Assume every representation has the same domain.#Different representations may show different restrictions.
=B. Check that notation, units, domain restrictions, and the final interpretation match the task.#Correct. These checks help confirm that the answer is mathematically valid for the stated task.
}

::PC_U01_L03_Q25::
Question ID: PC_U01_L03_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.4
Question:
Which error should be avoided when working with Radical and Rational Function Models
{
~A. Writing the given information first.#Writing givens first is a helpful setup, not the error.
~B. Explaining why a theorem, identity, or model applies.#Explaining why a method applies strengthens the solution.
=C. Using an unrelated formula or visual pattern without checking the lesson conditions.#Correct. Appearance alone is not reliable; the method must match the conditions in the lesson.
~D. Checking the final answer in the context of the problem.#Checking in context is a good habit, not an error.}
